  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nAn active synoptic pattern is present across the CONUS early this \nmorning with remnants of Isaias lifting north over the Tennessee \nValley, post-tropical cyclone Rachel approaching southern \nCalifornia, and a closed low across the Pacific Northwest. The \nforecast area remains wedged in between all of these features, \nleading to continued dry and abnormally warm conditions. Expecting \nalmost a carbon-copy of yesterday for today, except with some \npassing high clouds. South-southwest will gust at 25-35 MPH and \nhighs will reach the upper 80s to around 90 degrees. Elevated winds \novernight tonight will limit radiational cooling and overnight lows \nare forecast to be in the upper 60s to low 70s. Slightly warmer \n850mb temperatures build overhead on Monday, allowing temperatures \nto rise a few more degrees, topping out in the low 90s.\n\nPredictability decreases dramatically beyond Monday. Guidance \nremains consistent in a surface boundary sagging into the area \nTuesday, but the southern progression of the front is uncertain. \nTemperatures Tuesday will be dependent on the location of the front; \nhighs will likely be near 90 degrees again ahead of the boundary, \nbut only around 70 degrees behind the front. There is a signal for \nbetter upper-level support to go along with frontal forcing Tuesday \nafternoon into Wednesday morning, leading to increasing chances for \nshowers and storms. A few storms could be strong to severe \nTuesday afternoon/evening given shear around 30 kts and MLCAPE \naround 1500 J/kg. Damaging wind gusts and large hail would be\nthe main hazards.\n\nEnsembles continue to exhibit inconsistency and variability with the \nevolution of the western closed low. There is essentially a 50-50 \nsplit among GEFS, ENS, and GEPS members: one-half holds the closed \nlow west of the forecast area through Thursday while the other half \nmoves an open wave towards the Plains. There has been a trend\ntowards the wave not entirely ejecting across the Plains and \nremaining farther west. Regardless of exact evolution, the \nfrontal boundary in the region along passing energy (either in \nthe form of the longwave trough or embedded waves in the \nsouthwesterly flow) will spark rounds of showers and storms \nWednesday and Thursday. There could be some severe potential \nduring this timeframe, but high uncertainty in the synoptic \npattern leads to very low mesoscale predictability. PWATs of \n1.5-1.75\" are progged to be in place, supporting efficient \nrainfall with showers and storms. Ensemble means for total \nrainfall through Friday are 0.5-1.5\" with highest amounts across\nsoutheast and east-central Kansas. The upper-end of guidance \nshows the potential for 2-3\" totals in some spots, which could \nlead to some instances of flooding. Need to see guidance come \ninto better agreement before becoming too concerned with the \nflooding and/or severe potential.\n\nA strong wave is progged to dive southeast across the northern and \neastern US late this week with surface high pressure building into \nthe central CONUS. While temperatures are likely to fall back to \nmore seasonable values, differences remain in exactly how much \ncooler it will be.",
